Background and aims: Malformations of cortical development
(MCD) are increasingly recognised as a major cause for pharma-
coresistant seizures, neurological deficits and cognitive distur-
bances. This study aimed at identifying spectrum and clinical fea-
tures of patients with MCD and epilepsy.
Methods: We identified 237 patients (127w/110m) aged 1-75
years (mean 31.19 years) with MCD and epilepsy at the Seizure
Unit, Department of Neurology, Innsbruck Medical University. All
patients were clinically examined, underwent EEG and MRI.
Results: MCD spectrum included 62 patients with focal cortical
dysplasia (FCD), 27 – ganglioglioma (GG), 8 – dysembryoplastic
neuroepithelial tumour (DNET), 26 – tuberous sclerosis (TS), 13 –
hemimegalencephaly (HMGE), 14 – microcephaly, 30 – periven-
tricular nodular heterotopia (PNH), 2 – lissencephaly/pachygyria,
6 – subcortical laminar heterotopia (SLH), 49 –polymicrogyria
(PMG). Mean age at seizure onset was 11.8 years (range 1 month
– 56 years). The majority of patients with microcephaly, HMGE
and TS had seizure onset during the first year of life, whereas in
most patients with FCD, GG and PNH seizures manifested during
the second decade of life. 150 (63%) patients had pharmacoresis-
tant seizures. 43 patients underwent epilepsy surgery and 26/43
(60.5%) have been seizure free for at least one year following sur-
gery. Neurological deficits (n=119; 50.2%), delayed developmen-
tal milestones (n=93; 39.2%) and cognitive impairment (n=101;
42.6%) were observed mainly in patients with microcephaly, TS,
HMGE and PMG.
Conclusions: The most frequent MCD in these large series was
FCD. The patients with microcephaly, HMGE, TS and PMG have
earlier onset of epilepsy and frequently suffer from neurological
and cognitive deficits.
