Background: Fabry disease (FD) is an X-linked lysosomal storage
disorder caused by alpha-galactosidase A deficiency. Its dominant
complication in the CNS is ischaemic stroke. The aim of this study
was to characterise stroke in FD, by gender, age, co-morbidities,
arterial territory, pathogenetic mechanisms, and outcome.
Methods: The Fabry outcome survey (FOS) is an international
database of FD patients. Baseline data in FOS permitted identifi-
cation of patients who had had an ischaemic stroke, including gen-
der, age and co-morbidities. Once these patients had been ascer-
tained, a questionnaire was sent to the appropriate centre. This
questionnaire asked about vascular territory, classification by the
TOAST criteria, and resultant disability using the modified Rankin
scale.
Results: By October 2007, 1182 adults had been enrolled in FOS
(578 men, 604 women) of who 103 had experienced an ischaemic
stroke (63 men, 40 women). The mean age at first stroke was 45.6
years (males), 54.1 years (females). Patients who had suffered a
stroke were likelier to have cardiac and/or renal disease than those
in FOS with no history of stroke (p<0.0001). Stroke questionnaires
were completed on 46 patients. The most frequent arterial territo-
ry affected was the vertebro-basilar. The commonest aetiology was
small-vessel occlusion (38%). The mean post-stroke disability was
3.3 on the Rankin scale.
Conclusions: Ischaemic stroke is common in FD patients, with a
male preponderance and early age at onset. A “typical” FD patient
with ischaemic stroke will have cardiac and renal co-morbidities,
and will have had a small-vessel event in the posterior circulation,
with resulting minor/moderate disability.
