Background: Deep intra-cerebral haemorrhage (ICH) is usual-
ly considered as the consequence of small-vessel disease due to
arterial hypertension (HTN) or diabetes mellitus, and lobar ICH
as the consequence of vascular malformations, amyloid
angiopathies. Therefore, patients with deep ICH tend to be less
investigated than those with lobar haemorrhage, especially when
they are old and hypertensive. There is however little evidence
supporting this classification. Our objective was to compare risk
factor profiles of patients with deep and lobar ICH.
Methods: We prospectively included 309 consecutive patients
(median age 71) admitted to the emergency department of the
Lille University hospital (11/2004 – 08/2007) for a supratentor-
ial ICH. The origin of the ICH was defined on the 1st CT -scan
performed at admission. Data were prospectively collected; cho-
lesterol dosages were performed within the first 48 hours.
Results: Of 309 ICH, 180 (57%) were deep and 129 (42%)
lobar. Both groups did not differ for pre-stroke HTN (65% in
deep vs. 64%; p=0.77), gender, diabetes, and smoking. Patients
with deep ICH were more likely to be alcoholic (32% vs. 21%;
p=0.04), and to have higher total cholesterol (1.99 g/l vs. 1.88;
p=0.03) and low-density lipoprotein cholesterol (1.16 g/l vs.
1.03; p=0.02) levels; they tended to be younger (68 years vs. 74;
p=0.08).
Conclusion: Deep and lobar ICH do not significantly differ for
most risk factors except cholesterol and alcohol. A decision to
guide the diagnosis work-up by age, location and presence of
arterial hypertension may therefore be inappropriate.
