Objective: Aim of the study was to investigate the 24-h rhythm of
body core temperature (BcT) under controlled condition in nar-
coleptic patients and healthy control subjects.
Methods: Ten narcoleptic patients with cataplexy (9 men; 37±11
years) and ten healthy subjects (7 men; 41±12 years) were studied.
Rectal temperature was measured every 2 minutes for 48 hours by
means of Mini-logger. During the study, subjects lived in a temper-
ature controlled room (24 ± 1° C) lying in bed except for eating six
times per day (1800 Kcal) in a fixed light-dark schedule (dark
period 11 PM to 7 AM). BcT rhythmicity was analysed on the indi-
vidual 2-days recording data according to the single cosinor
method evaluating the presence of a 24-h rhythm, the mesor
(MES), amplitude (AMP) and acrophase (ACR) of the rhythm.
Statistical analysis was performed using unpaired t-test of group
means.
Results: Narcoleptics and controls showed a significant 24-h BcT
rhythm. No differences between groups were found in MES, AMP
and ACR; narcoleptics showed a significant (p<0.01) dispersal of
ACR values (controls: 16.47±1.02 h; narcoleptics: 17.14±1.55 h)
and a significant lower minimum nocturnal temperature (p<0.05).
Conclusions: Under controlled condition, BcT rhythm of nar-
coleptic patients did not differ significantly from the control group
for cosinor fitting. Narcoleptic patients showed a wider significant
variability in ACR values and a significant deeper nocturnal tem-
perature decline. Further studies are needed to evaluate sleep
stages-dependent changes of BcT.
