The autonomic nervous system (ANS) is an important modulator
of paroxysmal atrial fibrillation (PAF). It is accepted that the clin-
ical pattern of vagally-mediated PAF occurs particularly in young
patients. Neurocardiogenic responses during orthostatic stress are
related to autonomic reflexes where the vagal influence predomi-
nates. In this work, we evaluate the susceptibility of elderly PAF-
patients to activate vasovagal syncope mechanisms. A group of
PAF patients (n=34, aged 72±7y, 62% women, 56% without struc-
tural heart disease) with >1 year of PAF without clinical history of
syncope and another group of patients with history of recurrent
syncope of unknown aetiology [SYN] (n=34, aged 74±6y, 53%
women, 62% without structural heart disease), without clinical
history of PAF were studied. Patients with sinus-node dysfunc-
tion, diabetes, congestive heart-failure or taking beta-blockers
were excluded. Patients were tilted-up (70º, 40 min), while on
sinus rhythm. No provocative agents were used. ECG and blood
pressure (BP) were continuously monitored. The test was consid-
ered positive when syncope/ presyncope occurred associated with
bradycardia and/or arterial hypotension (and, classified as car-
dioinhibitory, vasodepressive or mixed-response). HUT was pos-
itive in 6 PAF patients (17.6% of PAF patients; 21% of patients
without heart-disease) and in 8 SYN patients (23.5% of SYN
patients; 33% of patients without heart-disease) (p=NS). Neuro-
cardiogenic responses were vasodepressive in 5 PAF patients and
7 SYN patients, (p=NS); mixed in 1patient of each group (p=NS).
During HUT, 3 PAF and 1 SYN patients had short periods of PAF
(p=NS). There were no differences in gender distribution, age or
heart disease. No orthostatic hypotension was observed. In elder-
ly PAF patients, a significant number of false positive results may
be expected during passive HUT due to increased vaso-vagal reac-
tions, despite aging. This suggests that ANS imbalances may be
observed in elderly PAF patients.
