This study discusses the assessment of cardiovascular autonom-
ic neuropathy (CAN) in type I diabetic patients and its correla-
tion with other diabetes complications. CAN detection based on
heart rate (HR) response to deep breathing, blood pressure (BP)
and HR response to a stand test was performed in 684 type I
patients (age: 43.2±14y – diabetes duration: 20.2±10.3y).
Patients considered as positive had an autonomic laboratory
evaluation to determine CAN severity according to the “Ewing
Score” (ES) based on: HR variations during controlled breath-
ing, stand test and Valsalva manoeuvre, BP variations during
orthostatic and hand grip tests. Gastrointestinal, bladder and
erectile dysfunction, peripheral neuropathy, retinopathy,
nephropathy and hypertension were also assessed. Non paramet-
ric tests and logistic regression model were used to identify the
patient characteristics most associated with CAN. 452 patients
(66.1%) had no significant abnormality (ES: 0–0.5), 148
patients (21.6%) had mild dysautonomia, mainly parasympa-
thetic (ES: 1–2) and 84 patients (12.3%) had confirmed auto-
nomic failure (ES>2). CAN severity was significantly correlat-
ed with age, diabetes duration (p<0.0001), HbA1C (p=0.0293)
and most of the other diabetes complications. However, in the
logistic regression model, the following factors remained to dis-
criminate the 3 levels of CAN severity: age, bladder dysfunction,
retinopathy, nephropathy, peripheral neuropathy, HTA and erec-
tile dysfunction. These results confirmed the high prevalence of
CAN in type I diabetic patients. Genito-urinary disorders, hyper-
tension and microangiopathic complications were the most asso-
ciated with CAN. Diabetes duration alone did not discriminate
between well patients with and without CAN.
