Background: Behçet’ s disease (BD) is a multisystem disorder
with recurrent oral and genital ulcerations, ocular involvement,
and in some, neurological manifestations. We describe 4 BD
patients with postural symptoms and syncopal episodes who
were diagnosed with the Postural Tachycardia Syndrome (PoTS).
Methods: We utilized established autonomic investigation
(head-up tilt, isometric exercise, mental arithmetic, cutaneous
cold, deep breathing, Valsalva manoeuvre, standing, 24hours
blood pressure and heart rate profile) (Mathias & Bannister,
2002) to assess 4 patients, who were referred with a confirmed
diagnosis of BD.
Results: None had orthostatic hypotension or evidence of sym-
pathetic vasoconstrictor or cardiac parasympathetic failure. In
all there was a postural heart rate rise over 30 beats/min during
postural challenge, fulfilling the criteria for the PoTS. These
findings were further confirmed by 24hr blood pressure and
heart rate profiles. All had normal supine plasma noradrenaline
and adrenaline levels; in 3 the noradrenaline response to postur-
al challenge was normal; in 1 a standing sample was not
obtained.
Conclusion: We report 4 BD in whom there was unequivocal
evidence for PoTS. BD has vascular and neurological effects,
either or both of which could be responsible for the PoTS. The
mechanisms accounting for PoTS, and whether it is common in
BD patients need to be established. Mathias CJ, Bannister R.
