Background and aims: It is unknown whether current immuno-
modulating therapy in relapsing-remitting MS delays secondary
progression. To evaluate this we stratified longitudinal data in
treated and untreated MS patients. We used predictors (afferent
or efferent onset episode, remission or not, mono- or polyfocal
onset) explaining much of the individual variability in time to
onset of secondary progression, and added therapy as a further
predictor.
Methods: We included an incidence cohort of 218 untreated
patients with relapsing-remitting MS and 903 patients with
relapsing-remitting MS from the Swedish MS registry treated
with interferon beta or Copaxone. Both data sets were stratified
in benign cases with 3 positive predictors, intermediate cases
with 1–2 positive predictors and severe cases without positive
predictors. We used a virtual placebo procedure based on hazard
functions calculated for each of the strata. Treated and untreated
patients were compared with a maximum likelihood estimate
within strata, and the overall treatment effect was evaluated with
Poisson regression.
Results: Of benign cases, 42.4% of untreated and 16.6% treated
patients changed to the secondary progressive phase during a
period of 10 years (p<0.001). Corresponding proportions were
48.7% and 24.2% in intermediate patients (p<0.001), and 85.7%
and 29.4% in severe cases (p=0.002). The effect of therapy over
all strata was a 38% lower risk for transition to secondary pro-
gression (p=0.0013).
Conclusions: Long-term immunomodulating therapy in the
relapsing-remitting phase has a protracted effect on the course of
MS, producing significant delay in the time of onset of second-
ary progression.
