Some patients with relapsing-remitting multiple sclerosis respond
excellently to treatment with corticosteroids, whilst others
respond poorly. To further inform the risk-benefit equation, it
would be useful to have a predictive score to determine, for an
individual patient suffering from a relapse, whether corticos-
teroids will or will not be useful. Using data (102 patients) gener-
ated from a completed randomised controlled trial {1} we have
developed a predictive model using binary logistic regression.
Based on clinically-driven apriori hypotheses we selected key
patient variables: age, gender, baseline EDSS/MSIS29 and time
interval between referral and treatment. Analysing those who
improved by EDSS/H113500.5 [B 6.761] we found that variables which
predicted a good response to corticosteroids were: younger age
(mean 39.1 vs. 43.3 years; 95% CI36.9–41.3, 40.0–46.5; [B-
0.059], p<0.02), less severe impairment (MSIS-29 physical, mean
63.7 vs. 75.4; 95% CI 58.5–68.8, 70.8–80.0; [B-0.042] p<0.003)
and earlier referral (mean 4.8 vs. 6.2 days; 95% CI 4.1–5.6,
4.7–6.0; [B-0.167], p<0.033). A similar conclusion was found for
the group with an EDSS improvement of /H113501.0 [B7.104]: mean
age 37.3 vs. 43.8 years (95% CI 41.2–46.4, 34.9–39.6; [B-0.100],
p<0.0001), MSIS-29 physical 64.3 vs. 68.2 (95% CI 57.9–70.6,
67.4–75.9; [B-0.028], p<0.024) and referral interval 4.5 vs. 6.1
days (95% CI 3.7–5.4, 5.2–7.0; [B-0.245], p<0.009). Both models
fitted well (69.6% and 71.6% respectively). Whilst the conclusion
is straightforward and somewhat self-evident, it emphasises the
need for earlier relapse treatment rather than watchful waiting.
