Background: Susceptibility to multiple sclerosis is genetically
determined but little is known about which genes are involved.
Objective: Our aim was to identify non-synonymous coding vari-
ants influencing the risk of developing multiple sclerosis.
Methods:In a Genome-Wide Association Study (GW AS) employing
14,436 non-synonymous SNPs typed in 975 cases and 1,466 controls
from the UK as part of the WTCCC we identified 18 potential asso-
ciated markers. Subsequently, we have extended the analysis of these
SNPs to additional samples from the UK, Belgium, the US and Nor-
way, ultimately involving a total of 1,681 trio families, 4,632 inde-
pendent cases and 4,454 controls (over 14,000 individuals in all).
Results: In the final analysis of all available data significant asso-
ciation was seen with the established susceptibility locus
rs6897932 (exon 6 of IL7R) p=7.0×10−7 and the novel variant
rs34536443 (exon 23 of TYK2) p=1.8×10−6.
Conclusion: The minor allele of the TYK2 (background frequen-
cy 4.7%) protects against the development of multiple sclerosis
with an Odds Ratio of 0.74 (0.66–0.84).
