Background: BCS with its characteristic MRI signature is
presently considered an MS variant. Most previous MRI investiga-
tions have not used highly typical BCS lesions. Therefore, an MRI
investigation was undertaken with assessment of pathology and
treatment in a BCS patient with unequivocally characteristic, con-
centrically banded white matter lesions.
Methods: Laboratory evaluation and a stereotaxic brain biopsy
were performed. MRI analysis utilized a 1.5 Tesla machine with
acquisition of T1-weighted, T2-weighted, proton density, FLAIR,
DWI and ADC images. MRI/MRS was performed pre-treatment,
and MRS thereafter. A course of PE (3 litres daily, 6 treat-
ments)/dexamethasone coverage was instituted after treatment fail-
ure clinically with i.v. methylprednisolone/oral prednisone.
Results: Laboratory studies including viral were negative. CSF: 7
lymphs, 2 polys, oligoclonal bands, elevated IgG index. Biopsy:
Numerous foamy macrophages, gemistocytes, and scattered lym-
phocytes within areas of axonal loss and demyelination. Within 1
week of completion of PE, significant improvement in neurological
function occurred and was maintained. FLAIR images demonstrat-
ed hyperintense and hypointense bands, but also isointense bands,
or more often an isointense area within an hypointense band. MRS
NAA/Cr ratio increased and Cho/Cr decreased suggesting improve-
ment in axonal and myelin integrity after treatment.
Conclusions: MRI analysis was compatible with alternating bands
of anti-inflammatory vasogenic oedema, and pro-inflammatory
cytotoxic oedema, the latter band containing different stages of cel-
lular apoptosis/necrosis. CSF and biopsy findings, and response to
PE suggest that BCS results from an autoimmune cascade with a
humoral component, which attacks primarily axons and oligoden-
drocytes causing apoptosis/necrosis.
